diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — "20.x machines and 120.x machines cannot reach each other" — is directly contradicted by live evidence. A topology-wide reachability probe shows pc0 (20.1.1.10) reaching pc12 (120.1.1.10) and the reverse with 0% packet loss in both directions.

The Kath5 topology deliberately operates two routing domains: OSPF (r1 through r10) and EIGRP (r12 and r13). Router r11 is the intentional translation point between them. On its OSPF side, r11 runs `redistribute connected` and `redistribute eigrp`, injecting the entire EIGRP domain (120.0.0.0/8, 102.x loopbacks, 103.x segment, 113.x loopbacks) as OSPF external routes so that the full OSPF backbone — including r1 — has valid forwarding entries for the 120.x side. On the EIGRP side, r12 and r13 carry pre-installed kernel static default routes (`K>* 0.0.0.0/0`) pointing back to r11 (112.1.1.1 and 103.1.1.1 respectively), providing the return path to the OSPF domain without requiring OSPF-into-EIGRP redistribution. This asymmetric design is complete and operational.

Phase A found no ACL rules, no duplicate MACs, no addressing faults. Phase B showed apparent OSPF flags for r12 and r13 that are false positives (those devices run EIGRP, not OSPF) and an r8 orphaned-area flag (area 0.0.0.2) that does not affect 20.x↔120.x forwarding because r8 functions as an ABR that correctly summarises its area-0.0.0.2 prefixes into area 0.0.0.1. Phase D pressure sweep was entirely clean. No fault mechanism, no historical trace, and no impacted traffic path was found.

The maintenance ticket's claim is unconfirmed by any current evidence. The network is healthy.
